Subject: [PATCH] Make the table formatter's header describe the table it heads

The "Table" formatter built a header row that did not describe the table it sat
on top of. Four things were wrong, and all of them are invisible in normal use
because the header row is `visually-hidden` -- they only ever surface for a
screen reader user or an audit. All four undermine WCAG 2.1 AA success
criterion 1.3.1 Info and Relationships.

1. No header cell carried a `scope` attribute, so nothing told assistive
   technology which header belonged to which cell.

2. The day cell was a plain `<td>`, so even with column headers an hours cell
   was announced with its column name and nothing tied it to its day -- which
   in a schedule is the association that carries the meaning. The existing
   `'header' => !$row_columns['label']` could never be TRUE, because that
   branch only runs when the same value is truthy, so the day was always a
   plain cell. It is now a `<th scope="row">`, and the CSS keeps its
   left alignment so it does not inherit the browser's centred `th` default.

3. The first header cell declared `colspan="3"` while the body rows have one
   cell per column, so a three-column table declared five columns.

4. `buildTableHeader()` and `buildTableRow()` each decided separately whether a
   'Day' column existed, on conditions that did not agree: the header only
   asked `$row_columns['label']`, the row also required
   `day_format !== 'none'`. At `day_format: none` with exceptions or seasons
   enabled -- a supported combination, `day_format` being a select with a
   `none` option -- the header therefore declared a column no row filled. The
   condition now lives in `$row_columns['label']` alone and both sides read it,
   so they cannot drift apart again.

The colspan existed to let the season / exception section title span the table,
but the other column headers were still emitted beside it, so it never spanned
anything -- it only inflated the declared column count. That title is a title
*for the table*, which is what `<caption>` is for, and `buildTable()` already
accepted one, so it moves there. Doing that also stops the title from
overwriting the 'Day' column header, which had left exception and season tables
with no header for their first column at all.

Moving the title is also what allows (4) to be fixed at the root. The
`|| $field_settings['exceptions'] || $field_settings['seasons']` half of
`$row_columns['label']` existed only to force a label column for the title to
live in. With the title in the caption it has no remaining purpose, and it was
the sole cause of the mismatch, so it goes.

`buildTable()`'s caption parameter is widened to accept `MarkupInterface`. The
exceptions title arrives as a `TranslatableMarkup` wrapping already-escaped
text; coercing it to `?string` would strip the interface that tells Twig the
value needs no further escaping, so a title containing an ampersand would
render as `&amp;`. That did not happen while the title lived in a header cell,
because `template_preprocess_table()` leaves a cell's `data` as an object.

The `$multiple` parameter of `buildTableHeader()` existed only to switch the
label cell between the section title and the 'Day' header. With the title in
the caption there is nothing left for it to decide, so it is dropped; both call
sites are in this file, and no subclass overrides the method.

The CSS rule that indented the section title follows it to the caption.

Reported against drupal/office_hours 8.x-1.29.
See https://github.com/yalesites-org/YaleSites-Internal/issues/1706

